  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly quiet through Wednesday night. \n\nSurface high pressure continues to influence southern New England's \nweather as it builds in from the Ohio valley this afternoon. Even \nso, a lingering mid level trough/cold pool aloft is leading to a \nsmattering of diurnal cumulus and hit-and-miss showers over \nwestern/central areas. With sunset these showers will \ndissipate and cloudcover quickly recede. Tonight expect clear skies \nwith light winds and patchy fog in the interior where dewpoint \ndepressions may be near 0. Wednesday is dry and sunny with mid level \nridging building in. As this happens, the low/mid level flow backs \nto the SW 850mb temps bump up from ~11C to 12-13C pushing high \ntemperatures a few degrees warmer on Wednesday, into the upper \n70s/low 80s with cooler temps along the coast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A round or two of scattered showers & thunderstorms \npossible Thu afternoon into Fri along with summerlike humidity as well.\n\nThe model guidance is in fairly good agreement in an approaching \nshortwave trough/cold front late in the work week. Out ahead of this \ndisturbance...S-SW flow will result in the return of low level \nmoisture and summerlike humidity. While specific timing remains \nuncertain...the potential exists for a round or two of scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms sometime Thu afternoon/night into Fri. The \nCSU machine learning probabilities also indicate a low risk for \nsevere weather/heavy rainfall. That is basically climatology for an \napproaching shortwave this time of year. But the question remains \ndoes the forcing/instability lineup favorably for that potential to \nbe realized along with various mesoscale processes. It is just too \nearly to determine that at this time. Nonetheless...certainly \nsomething to watch over the next day or two.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Mainly dry and seasonable weather follows for the \nweekend. Temps may trend above normal early next week. \n\nHigh pressure builds in behind the departing shortwave/cold front \nfor the upcoming weekend. The result will be mainly dry and \nseasonable weather Sat into Sun. Ensembles indicating upper level \nflow may become more westerly early next week allowing for temps to \ntrend above normal.",
